Useful script for duplicating the HighlandApp database:

```sql
-- *** 1. Configuration Variables (!! CHECK THESE LINES !!) ***
DECLARE @SourceDBName sysname = 'HighlandApp';
DECLARE @NewDBName sysname = 'TTDSTest';
DECLARE @BackupPath nvarchar(260) = '/var/opt/mssql/data/temp_copy_backup.bak';
DECLARE @DataDir nvarchar(260) = '/var/opt/mssql/data/';

-- Variables to hold the full, concatenated path strings
DECLARE @NewDataFilePath nvarchar(260);
DECLARE @NewLogFilePath nvarchar(260);

DECLARE @LogicalDataName sysname = @SourceDBName;        -- 'HighlandApp'
DECLARE @LogicalLogName sysname = @SourceDBName + N'_log'; -- 'HighlandApp_log'


-- *** 2. Perform the Full Backup to the Temporary File ***
PRINT N'Starting Full Backup of ' + @SourceDBName + N'...';
BACKUP DATABASE @SourceDBName
TO DISK = @BackupPath
WITH INIT,
     COMPRESSION;
PRINT N'Backup Complete to: ' + @BackupPath;


-- *** 3. Build New Physical File Paths ***
SET @NewDataFilePath = @DataDir + @NewDBName + '_Data.mdf';
SET @NewLogFilePath  = @DataDir + @NewDBName + '_Log.ldf';


-- *** 4. RESTORE DATABASE (The Copy) ***
PRINT N'Starting Restore to create new database: ' + @NewDBName;
PRINT N'Using Logical Data Name: ' + @LogicalDataName;
PRINT N'New Data File Path: ' + @NewDataFilePath;

-- Use the pre-set Logical Names and pre-concatenated physical paths in the MOVE commands
RESTORE DATABASE @NewDBName
FROM DISK = @BackupPath
WITH MOVE @LogicalDataName TO @NewDataFilePath,
     MOVE @LogicalLogName TO @NewLogFilePath,
     NOUNLOAD,
     REPLACE;

-- *** 5. Final Status and Cleanup Reminder ***
SELECT N'Database Copy Complete!' AS Status, @NewDBName AS NewDatabase;
SELECT N'*** REMINDER: Manually delete the temporary backup file: ' + @BackupPath + N' ***' AS CleanupNote;
```

I set it up in Vercel, and it was working great, except that there was no auth (!) so I had to delete it.

Claude suggests starting with basic auth, something like this:

```typescript
import type { Handle } from '@sveltejs/kit';

export const handle: Handle = async ({ event, resolve }) => {
	const auth = event.request.headers.get('authorization');
	
	if (auth !== 'Basic ' + btoa('username:password')) {
		return new Response('Unauthorized', {
			status: 401,
			headers: {
				'WWW-Authenticate': 'Basic realm="Secure Area"'
			}
		});
	}
	
	return resolve(event);
};
```
(Where `username:password` is pulled from env vars)

That could work. I think I would rather set up OIDC, but that's a bigger thing.

Info from Claude about how to do that:

---
Google Cloud Console setup:

Go to https://console.cloud.google.com
Create a new project (or use existing)
Navigate to "APIs & Services" → "Credentials"
Click "Create Credentials" → "OAuth 2.0 Client ID"
Configure consent screen (just needs app name, your email)
Create OAuth client:

Application type: Web application
Authorized redirect URIs: http://localhost:5173/auth/callback/google (dev) and https://yourapp.vercel.app/auth/callback/google (prod)


Copy the Client ID and Client Secret

Takes ~5 minutes. The consent screen can stay in "testing" mode, which lets you add specific Google accounts (like just yours) without going through verification.

And then using `auth.js`:

```
npm install @auth/core @auth/sveltekit
```

In middleware:

```typescript
import { SvelteKitAuth } from "@auth/sveltekit"
import Google from "@auth/sveltekit/providers/google"

export const handle = SvelteKitAuth({
  providers: [Google],
})
```

Add to `.env`:
```
AUTH_SECRET=<generate random string>
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=<from Google Cloud Console>
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=<from Google Cloud Console>
```

In each route (I guess?):
```typescript
export const load = async (event) => {
  const session = await event.locals.auth();
  if (!session?.user) throw redirect(303, '/auth/signin');
  // your code
}
```
